# last_verified: 2026-09-29 · bash 5.3.20 · ShellCheck 0.11.0

<markdown_cell>
# Bash 5.2 → 5.3 migration — hands-on checklist

A runnable companion to `bash/docs/bash-5.3-migration-guide.md`. Each section exercises one behavior change under Bash 5.3 so the impact is observable before a runtime is swapped on a fleet. Run the code cells in order; cells that depend on 5.3-only syntax are tagged `Requires: Bash 5.3`. The prose guide holds the full reference; this notebook is the verification pass.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Purpose

Upgrading the Bash runtime touches three areas that break silently: command substitution (new in-shell forms that change exit-status propagation), pathname expansion ordering (`GLOBSORT`), and `test`/`[` precedence for parenthesized subexpressions. This notebook verifies each area by running a minimal repro and comparing the observed result against the documented 5.3 behavior, so a migration can be gated in CI rather than discovered in a post-upgrade pager alert.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## When to use

| Situation | Action |
|---|---|
| Auditing scripts for a 5.2 → 5.3 upgrade | Run every code cell; compare output to the "Expected" comments. |
| CI on a mixed-version fleet | Gate jobs that use 5.3-only syntax on `BASH_VERSINFO` checks (see cell 0). |
| Rewriting a subshell-lossy `$(...)` call site | Replace with `${ cmd; }` once 5.3 is the minimum runtime. |
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Prerequisites

- Bash 5.3 interpreter (`bash --version`).
- ShellCheck 0.11.0 or later — earlier versions do not parse `${| cmd; }` and emit spurious errors on the 5.3-only cells.
- A scratch directory writable by the current user (used by the glob-sort fixture).
- The prose reference at `bash/docs/bash-5.3-migration-guide.md` for full context on each change.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## 0. Version guard for mixed fleets

A CI worker still on 5.2 must not attempt 5.3-only syntax. The guard below fails the job fast with a clear message instead of a mid-script parse error.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# last_verified: 2026-09-29 · bash 5.3.20

if [ "${BASH_VERSINFO[0]}" -lt 5 ] || { [ "${BASH_VERSINFO[0]}" -eq 5 ] && [ "${BASH_VERSINFO[1]}" -lt 3 ]; }; then
    echo "abort: Bash 5.3+ required for this notebook" >&2
    exit 1
fi
echo "running on Bash ${BASH_VERSINFO[0]}.${BASH_VERSINFO[1]}"
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## 1. Command substitution: `${ command; }` and `${| command; }`

Bash 5.3 adds two in-shell command-substitution forms. Unlike `$(cmd)` — which forks a subshell and therefore discards side effects on shell variables — `${ cmd; }` executes in the current shell and `${| cmd; }` executes in the current shell and stores stdout in `REPLY`. The observable difference is whether a counter incremented inside the substitution survives outside it.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Requires: Bash 5.3
# last_verified: 2026-09-29 · bash 5.3.20

counter=0
bump() { counter=$((counter + 1)); echo "inc -> $counter"; }

# $(...) forks a subshell: the increment never reaches this shell.
out=$(bump)
echo "subshell  \$():    counter=$counter"

# ${ cmd; } runs in THIS shell: the increment persists.
out=${ bump; }
echo "current   \${ }:    counter=$counter"

# ${| cmd; } runs in THIS shell, stdout is placed in REPLY.
${| bump; }
echo "REPLY=$REPLY  counter=$counter"
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
### Expected under 5.3

```
subshell  $():    counter=0
current   ${ }:    counter=1
REPLY=inc -> 1  counter=2
```

Under 5.2, the `${ bump; }` and `${| bump; }` lines are syntax errors.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## 2. Pathname expansion ordering with `GLOBSORT`

Default pathname expansion orders matches by byte value (LC_COLLATE-dependent), so `10-access.log` sorts before `2-access.log`. The new `globsort` shopt switches expansion to lexical dictionary order — the ordering humans reading sorted filenames expect and the ordering CI diff tools usually assume.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Requires: Bash 5.3 (shopt -s globsort)
# last_verified: 2026-09-29 · bash 5.3.20

work=$(mktemp -d)
cd "$work" || exit 1
touch 1-access.log 2-access.log 10-access.log

shopt -u globsort  # default ordering (byte value)
printf 'byte order:  '; for f in *.log; do printf '%s ' "$f"; done; printf '\n'

shopt -s globsort   # lexical / dictionary order
printf 'globsort:    '; for f in *.log; do printf '%s ' "$f"; done; printf '\n'

cd - >/dev/null
rm -rf "$work"
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
### Expected under 5.3

```
byte order:  1-access.log 10-access.log 2-access.log
globsort:    1-access.log 2-access.log 10-access.log
```
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## 3. `test` builtin precedence (parenthesized subexpressions)

With more than four arguments, `test`/`[` groups parenthesized subexpressions differently than 5.2 did. Scripts that rely on the old grouping can flip from a match to a no-match without any syntax error. The portable fix is `[[ ... ]]`, whose precedence is unchanged.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# last_verified: 2026-09-29 · bash 5.3.20

a=5; b=3
# shellcheck disable=SC2166  # legacy [ -a ] with parens is the gotcha under demonstration
if [ \( "$a" -gt 0 \) -a \( "$b" -lt 10 \) ]; then
    echo "test matched"
fi
# Portable form — unaffected by the precedence change.
if [[ "$a" -gt 0 && "$b" -lt 10 ]]; then
    echo "[[ ]] matched"
fi
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
### Expected

Both branches print (a match). The lesson is the form, not the result: standardize on `[[ ]]` for compound tests so behavior does not depend on the runtime version's argument-count parsing.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## 4. Breaking-changes overview

- **In-shell command substitution** (`${ cmd; }`, `${| cmd; }`) — new syntax; 5.2 rejects both forms.
- **`GLOBSORT`** new shopt — absent in 5.2 (`shopt -s globsort` aborts there); off by default in 5.3, enable explicitly for deterministic ordering.
- **`test`/`[` precedence** for parenthesized subexpressions with more than four arguments — behavioral change, no syntax error.
- **`source -p`** now honors POSIX mode (`set -o posix`); a script that sourced a file expecting POSIX mode to be ignored may now see option changes take effect.
- **`MULTIPLE_COPROCS`** is on by default — several named `coproc` blocks can coexist, where 5.2 allowed one outstanding coprocess at a time.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Requires: Bash 5.3 (MULTIPLE_COPROCS is on by default)
# last_verified: 2026-09-29 · bash 5.3.20

coproc C1 { for i in 1 2 3; do echo "c1-$i"; done; }
coproc C2 { for i in a b c; do echo "c2-$i"; done; }

while read -r line; do echo "c1: $line"; done <"${C1[0]}"
while read -r line; do echo "c2: $line"; done <"${C2[0]}"
wait
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
### Expected under 5.3

```
c1: 1 c1: 2 c1: 3
c2: a c2: b c2: c
```

Under 5.2, declaring `C2` while `C1` is outstanding fails or shadows `C1`.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Verify

1. Confirm the interpreter: `bash --version` prints a 5.3 patchlevel.
2. Run cells 1–4; each "Expected" block matches the printed output.
3. Lint with ShellCheck 0.11.0 (`shellcheck -x -S error` on each cell's code) — cell 0, 3, and 4 are also clean under 0.10; the 5.3-only cells (1, 2) require 0.11.
4. In CI, gate 5.3-only syntax behind the version guard (cell 0) so a 5.2 runner fails the job instead of failing mid-script.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Common errors

- **Treating `${ cmd; }` as a subshell optimizer only**: it also changes exit-status propagation. A failing command inside `${ cmd; }` now sets `$?` in the current shell; code that ignored `$?` under `$(...)` may branch differently.
- **Enabling `globsort` globally without auditing consumers**: any script that parsed `ls`/`find` output assuming byte order will see reordered input. Enable per-script, not in a shared profile, until callers are validated.
- **Mixing `[` and `[[ ]]` in a codebase**: the precedence difference is silent. Standardize on `[[ ]]` for compound tests so a 5.2/5.3 diff shows no behavior change.
- **Assuming `coproc` names are still uniqueness-checked**: in 5.3 the uniqueness rule is relaxed, so two scripts both naming a coprocess `C1` will no longer collide loudly — pick namespacing that avoids silent reuse.
</markdown_cell>

